  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nBehind yesterday's cold front, surface high pressure noses into the \narea and continues affecting us with drier conditions and below \nnormal temperatures until Saturday. Looking at NAEFS climatology \nbetween mid July and early August on the Ensemble Sit Table, 850 mb \ntemperatures cooler than 8C happens about once in every 10 yrs or so \nin parts of N IN and NW OH midday today. The coolest night in this \nperiod is tonight and then we slowly warm with lows back above 60 \ndegrees again Saturday night. \n\nA strong theta-e plume pushes back through the area midday Saturday \nwith 70 degree dew points returning overnight and 850 mb \ntemperatures achieving 18+C again by Sunday morning. At this point, \nwe have a ridge over the western US and a trough over the eastern \nUS, putting our area in a northwest flow regime, which would seem to \nindicate the possibility of MCSs or ridge riders. Sunday morning has \nwhat appears to be an outflow boundary or at least some sort of \ntemperature gradient entering the area. Then there appears to be a \nmore concrete upper PV anomaly associated with upper divergence and \nsfc convergence on the GFS (with some model confluence from the \nECMWF with having a similar PV anomaly moving through Sunday \nmorning) that usually occurs in the vicinity of MCSs perhaps \nindicating the potential for such an occurrence Sunday afternoon \ndepending on the state of the area after the morning outflow \nboundary. We actually have some effective shear around on Saturday \ninto Saturday night, but instability appears rather weak at less \nthan 1000 J/kg. Shear is gone by the time we get into Sunday though. \nPerhaps another opportunity for stronger storms is some time on \nMonday. The GFS has better shear in the morning with thermal \nboundaries around as well as instability. The WxNext AI model \nindicates the best chance for severe weather is that Monday time \nframe.",
